{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-866be77266501b87","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Prompt Writing Guide\n2\t\n3\tA prompt is a markdown file that tells the YOLO agent what to build. Each prompt produces one atomic, verifiable change.\n4\t\n5\t## When to Write a Prompt\n6\t\n7\t**This document does NOT decide direct vs prompt vs spec.** That decision lives in [../choosing-a-flow.md](../choosing-a-flow.md) — the single source of truth. Read it first to confirm a prompt is the right flow.\n8\t\n9\tIn short: write a prompt when the change is a code change that does NOT carry a business-level \"why\" that deserves its own document. Markdown / docs / configs / small operator scripts → direct edits, not prompts. Features with a real business-why → spec first. See the canonical decision doc.\n10\t\n11\tYou may also write prompts manually for finer control over decomposition from an approved spec (the daemon does this automatically by default).\n12\t\n13\t## Creating a Prompt\n14\t\n15\tUse the Claude Code command:\n16\t\n17\t```\n18\t/dark-factory:create-prompt\n19\t```\n20\t\n21\tOr create manually. Location depends on status and project config:\n22\t\n23\t| Status | Directory | Purpose |\n24\t|--------|-----------|---------|\n25\t| `idea` | `prompts/ideas/` | Rough concepts, not ready for approval |\n26\t| `draft` | inbox dir (default: `prompts/`) | Complete prompts, ready for review and approval |\n27\t\n28\tThe inbox directory is configurable per project. Run `dark-factory config` to check the actual `inboxDir` path before writing prompts.\n29\t\n30\t```bash\n31\t# Check your project's inbox directory\n32\tdark-factory config | grep inboxDir\n33\t\n34\t# Idea — park it for later\n35\ttouch prompts/ideas/my-change.md\n36\t\n37\t# Draft — ready for review (use the inboxDir from config)\n38\ttouch prompts/my-change.md\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\tUse lowercase-kebab-case. Dark-factory assigns execution numbers on approve (e.g., `248-spec-044-model.md`).\n42\t\n43\t**Ordering prefix for dependent prompts.** When multiple prompts must execute in strict order, prefix with a simple number to define the sequence: `1-spec-044-model.md`, `2-spec-044-executor-timeout.md`, `3-spec-044-processor-retry.md`. Approve them in order — dark-factory replaces the prefix with execution numbers. Do NOT number standalone prompts.\n44\t\n45\t## Prompt Structure\n46\t\n47\t### Frontmatter\n48\t\n49\t```yaml\n50\t---\n51\tstatus: draft\n52\t---\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\tWhen linking to a spec:\n56\t\n57\t```yaml\n58\t---\n59\tspec: [\"030-foo-bar-baz\"]\n60\tstatus: draft\n61\tcreated: \"2026-03-11T10:00:00Z\"\n62\t---\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\t- `spec` must be a YAML array of strings — single or multiple entries\n66\t- **Canonical form is the full slug** (e.g. `[\"030-foo-bar-baz\"]`), not the bare number. Daemon-generated prompts use this form, and `pkg/slugmigrator` rewrites bare numbers to full slugs after each generation cycle so all prompts converge on the canonical form\n67\t- Bare numbers (`spec: [\"030\"]`) are accepted as input — the slug migrator resolves them to the full form at the next daemon iteration\n68\t- Only use `spec`, `status`, `created`, `issue` — dark-factory adds the rest\n69\t- Valid inbox statuses: `idea` (rough concept, needs refinement) or `draft` (complete, ready for approval)\n70\t\n71\t### Body\n72\t\n73\t```xml\n74\t<summary>\n75\t5-10 bullet points describing WHAT this prompt achieves, not HOW.\n76\tWritten for human review — no file paths, no struct names.\n77\t\n78\tGOOD:\n79\t- Projects can configure a validation command for all prompts\n80\t- The command's exit code determines success or failure\n81\t- Existing prompts continue to work unchanged\n82\t\n83\tBAD:\n84\t- Adds `validationCommand` field to `Config` in `pkg/config/config.go`\n85\t- Calls `exec.Command` in `pkg/executor/executor.go`\n86\t</summary>\n87\t\n88\t<objective>\n89\tWhat to build and why (1-3 sentences). State the end state, not the steps.\n90\t</objective>\n91\t\n92\t<context>\n93\tRead CLAUDE.md for project conventions.\n94\tRead `pkg/config/config.go` — find the `Config` struct and `Validate` method.\n95\t</context>\n96\t\n97\t<requirements>\n98\t1. Specific, numbered, unambiguous steps\n99\t2. Include exact file paths (repo-relative, not absolute)\n100\t3. Include function/type names as anchors (not line numbers)\n101\t4. Show old → new code patterns where helpful\n102\t5. End with a self-check: before finishing, re-run `<verification>` and confirm it passes; walk each acceptance criterion against the change\n103\t</requirements>\n104\t\n105\t<constraints>\n106\t- Do NOT commit — dark-factory handles git\n107\t- Existing tests must still pass\n108\t- [Copy constraints from spec — agent has no memory between prompts]\n109\t</constraints>\n110\t\n111\t<verification>\n112\tRun `make precommit` -- must pass.\n113\t</verification>\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\t## `<verification>` is container-only — never operator-side\n117\t\n118\tThe prompt's `<verification>` block runs INSIDE the YOLO container. The container has: the mounted repo, `make`, language runtimes, filesystem tools (`find`, `grep`), and network read (via `tinyproxy`) for its own remote.\n119\t\n120\tThe container does NOT have: a Docker socket, the `dark-factory` CLI, cluster credentials, `kubectl*` wrappers, host scripts that shell out to Docker or the network (`scripts/*.sh` in most bborbe projects), write access to any remote other than the project's own, and — if `.dark-factory.yaml` sets `hideGit: true` or `workflow: worktree` — a working `.git` directory.\n121\t\n122\t**Container-executable commands (safe in `<verification>`):**\n123\t\n124\t- `make precommit`, `make test`, `make check`, `make ensure`\n125\t- `grep`, `find`, `ls`, `cat` — filesystem checks for expected side-effects\n126\t- Language-specific test runners (`go test ./...`, `pytest`, `pnpm test`) that don't shell out to Docker\n127\t- The change's own binary / CLI if it was built in `<requirements>`\n128\t\n129\t**Operator-executable commands (NEVER in `<verification>`):**\n130\t\n131\t- `docker` (no socket), `make build` / `make build-multiarch` / `make buca` (shell out to Docker)\n132\t- `dark-factory <cmd>` (binary not in container PATH)\n133\t- `kubectl*`, `kubectlquant*`, `kubectlprod*`, `kubectldev*` (no cluster creds)\n134\t- `scripts/*.sh` when the script requires Docker or the network beyond `tinyproxy`\n135\t- `gh pr create`, `gh release`, `gh api` (no PAT with write scope inside container)\n136\t- `git ` (bare — trailing space avoids `github`/`git@`) when `hideGit: true` or `workflow: worktree` masks `.git` — the command dies with `fatal: not a git repository`\n137\t\n138\t**Why this matters — the false-positive-pass failure mode:** the daemon's executor does NOT check `<verification>` command exit codes for failure. A `git` command that dies with `fatal: not a git repository`, or a `docker` command that dies with `command not found`, still ships. The prompt lands, the code is committed, the verification never actually ran. This is the highest-signal reason to keep operator-only commands out of prompts.\n139\t\n140\t**If the check IS operator-only** (image build, dev cluster deploy, dark-factory healthcheck, admin gateway probe), move it to the spec's `# Verification` § \"Operator-executable\" rung — see `docs/rules/spec-writing.md` § \"Verification: container-executable vs operator-executable\". Never embed operator-only commands in the prompt's `<verification>` block or inside a labelled `NOTE FOR OPERATOR` sub-block in `<constraints>` — the auditor rejects both patterns as Critical.\n141\t\n142\t**If the container really can't verify anything meaningful for this change** — the change is likely spec-scale (its verification is E2E), and the flow decision should be revisited per `../choosing-a-flow.md`.\n143\t\n144\t## Writing Rules\n145\t\n146\t### Specificity over brevity — but pick the right *kind* of specificity\n147\t\n148\tLonger, more specific prompts succeed more often *when the specificity is about contracts and anchors*, not when it's about pre-deciding every line of code the agent will write. Spelling out every if/else and every error message ships the author's bugs verbatim and prevents the agent from applying existing project conventions. See [Detail Levels](#detail-levels) below for how to pick the right grain.\n149\t\n150\tAlways include:\n151\t- Exact file paths (repo-relative)\n152\t- Function/type names as primary anchors\n153\t- Library import paths\n154\t- References to existing files demonstrating each pattern the new code follows\n155\t\n156\tOptional (depends on detail level):\n157\t- Line numbers only as hints (`~line 176`) — go stale quickly\n158\t- Old → new code patterns for find-and-replace — useful for mechanical refactors, harmful for novel work\n159\t- Inlined code bodies — see Detail Levels for when this helps vs. hurts\n160\t\n161\t### Detail Levels\n162\t\n163\tPrompts span a spectrum from \"fully scripted\" to \"intent only.\" Each level has real trade-offs. Pick deliberately; default to **Level 3 (Medium)** for most work in a mature codebase. Mismatched grain is the most common cause of prompt failure: too much detail ships author bugs, too little produces wrong abstractions.\n164\t\n165\t| Level | Shape | Typical line count |\n166\t|-------|-------|-------------------|\n167\t| 1 — Very Detailed | Full inlined function bodies, every `if`/`else` written, error messages pre-decided | 500–1500 |\n168\t| 2 — Detailed | Function signatures + key error paths inlined; agent fills bodies | 200–500 |\n169\t| 3 — Medium *(default)* | Interfaces + sequence + pattern references + failure modes; no function bodies | 80–200 |\n170\t| 4 — Soft | End-state contracts + pattern references; agent chooses structure | 40–100 |\n171\t| 5 — Very Rough | One-paragraph intent + verification command | 10–40 |\n172\t\n173\t#### Level 1 — Very Detailed (scripted)\n174\t\n175\t**Shape:** Full Go source inlined for every function. Every conditional written out. Test cases enumerated by literal value.\n176\t\n177\t**Pros:**\n178\t- Maximum reproducibility across model versions; works on cheap/weak models.\n179\t- The exact output is in the prompt — no agent interpretation needed.\n180\t- Good for teaching examples or documenting a canonical implementation.\n181\t\n182\t**Cons:**\n183\t- The author's bugs ship verbatim (the agent faithfully implements the wrong `fmt.Errorf` instead of the project's `errors.Wrapf`).\n184\t- Drifts from project style because the prompt-author writes from memory, not from existing code.\n185\t- Audit becomes line-by-line code review of the prompt — slow and error-prone.\n186\t- The agent's pattern-matching strength is wasted.\n187\t- Any reusable knowledge inside the prompt rots and re-rots across every future prompt that needs the same pattern.\n188\t\n189\t**When to use:**\n190\t- Brand-new pattern with no existing exemplar in the codebase.\n191\t- Absolute first prompt of a new project (no convention to follow yet).\n192\t- Reproducing a published reference implementation that should match an external source line-for-line.\n193\t\n194\t#### Level 2 — Detailed (annotated skeleton)\n195\t\n196\t**Shape:** Function signatures and key error paths spelled out. Imports listed. Bodies left to the agent with hints (`// retry once on transient errors per DB#8`).\n197\t\n198\t**Pros:**\n199\t- Still very prescriptive about the contract surface.\n200\t- Manageable to audit (200–500 lines, not 1500).\n201\t- Less prone to style drift than Level 1 because bodies follow project conventions.\n202\t\n203\t**Cons:**\n204\t- The author still owns most of the logic; bugs in the spelled-out error paths still ship.\n205\t- The skeleton can subtly bias the agent away from a cleaner structure it would have chosen.\n206\t\n207\t**When to use:**\n208\t- Novel logic in a familiar project (the pattern doesn't exist yet, but the style does).\n209\t- Complex algorithms where the exact shape of the control flow matters.\n210\t- Migrating off a stable external API where the call sequence is fixed.\n211\t\n212\t#### Level 3 — Medium (contracts + references) — **default**\n213\t\n214\t**Shape:** Interface signatures, sequence of operations, pattern references (`see pkg/github/client.go for the errors.Wrapf style`), explicit failure modes copied from the spec, test scenarios named (`table-test the verdict × autoApprove matrix`). No function bodies. No pre-written error messages.\n215\t\n216\t**Pros:**\n217\t- Best for translation work — applying an existing project pattern to a new feature.\n218\t- Leverages the agent's pattern-matching strength: it reads the referenced file and adopts the style automatically.\n219\t- Small enough to audit at a contract level instead of a line-by-line review.\n220\t- Prompts age well — the contract changes rarely, the implementation can be regenerated.\n221\t- Author bugs in inlined code can't ship because there is no inlined code.\n222\t\n223\t**Cons:**\n224\t- Requires at least one good in-tree exemplar for each pattern referenced.\n225\t- Weaker models may produce non-uniform code across siblings without explicit shape.\n226\t- Reviewer must trust the contract rather than the implementation.\n227\t\n228\t**When to use:**\n229\t- Most refactors and feature work in mature codebases (the default).\n230\t- Multi-prompt specs where consistency across siblings matters.\n231\t- Any case where existing code already demonstrates the conventions the new code should follow.\n232\t\n233\t#### Level 4 — Soft (constraints + goals)\n234\t\n235\t**Shape:** What must be true at the end, plus references and constraints. No interfaces enumerated. Agent decides sequence, structure, and decomposition within the file.\n236\t\n237\t**Pros:**\n238\t- Maximum agent agency — it can refactor adjacent code if that produces a cleaner result.\n239\t- Very small prompts; fast to write.\n240\t- The agent may discover a better abstraction than the author would have specified.\n241\t\n242\t**Cons:**\n243\t- Outcomes more variable across runs and model versions.\n244\t- Relies on agent capability and self-discipline; weaker models drift.\n245\t- Harder to review (less to compare against).\n246\t- Multi-prompt coordination breaks down — siblings may make incompatible decisions.\n247\t\n248\t**When to use:**\n249\t- Greenfield work with a capable model.\n250\t- Rapid iteration when the right shape is uncertain.\n251\t- Cleanup tasks where the agent is expected to use judgment about how far to refactor.\n252\t\n253\t#### Level 5 — Very Rough (intent)\n254\t\n255\t**Shape:** One paragraph stating intent. A single verification command. No constraints, no references.\n256\t\n257\t**Pros:**\n258\t- Trivial to write — minutes from idea to attempt.\n259\t- Forces the agent to do the design thinking.\n260\t- Useful as a \"first draft\" that can be refined into Level 3.\n261\t\n262\t**Cons:**\n263\t- Highly variable output; often produces wrong abstractions on first try.\n264\t- Usually needs multiple rounds with edits — total wall time is often longer than starting at Level 3.\n265\t- Almost never satisfies the audit DoD on first pass.\n266\t- No reproducibility — same prompt twice produces different code.\n267\t\n268\t**When to use:**\n269\t- Spike / exploration where the answer isn't yet known.\n270\t- Throwaway scripts.\n271\t- Rapidly evolving requirements where any specification will be out of date by the time it's written.\n272\t\n273\t### Choosing a level\n274\t\n275\t**The Level 3 default assumes documented patterns actually exist in the codebase.** In a messy or greenfield codebase with no stable exemplars, Level 3 is dishonest — the prompt promises \"see X for the style\" but X doesn't exist, and the agent silently slides back into inlining. If patterns aren't there yet, Level 2 (spelled-out signatures, hinted bodies) is more honest until a pattern is proven, then promote to Level 3.\n276\t\n277\t**Step 0 — Discover existing patterns before writing.** Spend ~2 minutes running searches like:\n278\t\n279\t```bash\n280\t# Error-wrapping style in this project\n281\trg -l 'errors\\.Wrapf|fmt\\.Errorf' pkg/ internal/ | head\n282\t\n283\t# HTTP client construction\n284\trg -l 'http\\.Client|net/http' pkg/ | head\n285\t\n286\t# Counterfeiter mock pattern\n287\trg -l 'counterfeiter:generate' pkg/ | head\n288\t\n289\t# Test framework\n290\trg -l 'ginkgo\\.|gomega\\.|testing\\.T' pkg/ | head\n291\t```\n292\t\n293\tIf a surface returns 5+ matches with a consistent pattern → Level 3 references work. If it returns 0–2 → that pattern is novel for this codebase; choose Level 2 or document the new convention in a project doc first.\n294\t\n295\t**Then ask in order:**\n296\t\n297\t1. **Did Step 0 find ≥5 matches with a consistent pattern for the surface you're touching?**\n298\t   - Yes → continue to question 2.\n299\t   - No → **patterns are missing or inconsistent.** Continue to question 4 (do NOT silently fall through to Level 3 — that's the trap).\n300\t2. **Is this a published external API that must match line-for-line?**\n301\t   - Yes → Level 1 (and link the external source).\n302\t   - No → **Level 3** (reference the exemplars from Step 0; don't re-inline). This is the common case for translation work in mature codebases.\n303\t3. *(unreachable from question 1; kept for symmetry with prior versions)*\n304\t4. **(Patterns missing.) Will the agent need to invent a novel structure?**\n305\t   - Yes → Level 4 or 5 (let it explore), then promote to Level 3 once the pattern is proven AND document it in `project/docs/`.\n306\t   - No (translation work but no exemplar yet) → **Level 2** (spelled-out signatures, hinted bodies) — more honest than fake Level 3 references that don't exist. Promote to Level 3 in a future prompt once the pattern is documented.\n307\t\n308\t**The \"fall-through-to-Level-3\" trap:** the most common mistake. Author runs no searches, picks Level 3 because it's the \"default\", references files that don't exist or don't demonstrate the pattern claimed. Agent silently inlines (because it has nothing to reference), and the prompt produces author-style code anyway — back to the original problem. Step 0 + question 1's hard split prevents this.\n309\t\n310\t### What the spectrum does NOT solve\n311\t\n312\tPattern-anchoring (Level 3) solves **convention-drift bugs** — the agent adopts the project's existing style automatically. It does **not** solve **logic bugs in genuinely novel code**. Examples that no exemplar would catch:\n313\t\n314\t- `io.LimitReader(resp.Body, 500)` placed BEFORE `json.Unmarshal` — truncates the body before parsing, but no existing file demonstrates the right ordering because no existing file has this exact concern.\n315\t- A retry classifier that maps \"POST returned 200 but review absent in GET\" to the wrong error class — the logic itself is wrong, not the style.\n316\t- An off-by-one in pagination, an inverted boolean, a swapped argument order.\n317\t\n318\tThese need **test cases the agent must satisfy** (spec acceptance criteria, failure-modes table, scenario tests) or **adversarial review** — they cannot be prevented by referring to exemplars. The detail spectrum reduces *one class* of bug; the other class needs spec-level rigor.\n319\t\n320\t### Worked example: bot-identity self-check at three levels\n321\t\n322\tSame surface (verify the GitHub bot account matches `BOT_GITHUB_LOGIN` before posting), three grain levels:\n323\t\n324\t**Level 1 — Very Detailed**\n325\t\n326\t```text\n327\t## Step 4 — Implement BotIdentityCheck\n328\t\n329\tCreate function `func CheckBotIdentity(ctx context.Context, client HTTPClient, expectedLogin string) error`:\n330\t\n331\t1. Build request:\n332\t   req, err := http.NewRequestWithContext(ctx, \"GET\", \"https://api.github.com/user\", nil)\n333\t   if err != nil {\n334\t       return fmt.Errorf(\"build request: %w\", err)\n335\t   }\n336\t   req.Header.Set(\"Authorization\", \"token \"+token)\n337\t   req.Header.Set(\"Accept\", \"application/vnd.github+json\")\n338\t\n339\t2. Execute request:\n340\t   resp, err := client.Do(req)\n341\t   if err != nil {\n342\t       return fmt.Errorf(\"execute request: %w\", err)\n343\t   }\n344\t   defer resp.Body.Close()\n345\t\n346\t[... 60 more lines of fully-written Go ...]\n347\t```\n348\t\n349\t**Cost:** The author wrote `fmt.Errorf` from memory. The project uses `errors.Wrapf(ctx, err, ...)`. The agent will faithfully ship `fmt.Errorf` — every test passes; `make precommit` lint fails or worse, the convention drifts silently.\n350\t\n351\t**Level 3 — Medium (default)**\n352\t\n353\t```text\n354\t## Bot identity check (DB#4 in spec 027)\n355\t\n356\tContract:\n357\t- Function: CheckBotIdentity(ctx, client HTTPClient, expectedLogin string) error\n358\t- Returns nil iff GitHub's GET /user response's `login` field equals expectedLogin\n359\t- Returns errors.Wrapf(ctx, ErrBotIdentityMismatch, \"got %q, want %q\", actual, expectedLogin) on mismatch\n360\t- Returns wrapped error on HTTP / parse failure per the retry-policy classification\n361\t\n362\tPattern references (read before writing):\n363\t- `pkg/github/client.go` for HTTP+errors.Wrapf style and Authorization header construction\n364\t- `pkg/githubauth/types.go` for the HTTPClient interface shape and Counterfeiter annotation\n365\t- `pkg/verdict.go` for sentinel-error pattern (see `ErrUnknownVerdict`)\n366\t\n367\tTest (use DescribeTable):\n368\t- happy: 200 + login=expectedLogin → nil\n369\t- mismatch: 200 + login=other → wrapped ErrBotIdentityMismatch\n370\t- transient: network error → wrapped per pkg/github/client.go classification\n371\t- malformed: non-JSON response → wrapped, treated as permanent\n372\t```\n373\t\n374\t**Cost:** None of the inlined bugs are possible — the agent reads `pkg/github/client.go` and adopts the existing `errors.Wrapf` style, header construction, and error classification. ~25 lines of prompt vs ~70.\n375\t\n376\t**Level 5 — Very Rough**\n377\t\n378\t```text\n379\t## Bot identity check\n380\t\n381\tThe agent self-checks that the GitHub PAT belongs to `my-bot-account`\n382\tbefore posting any review. Mismatch → refuse + diagnostic.\n383\t\n384\tverification: make precommit\n385\t```\n386\t\n387\t**Cost:** The agent might invent the function in the wrong package, miss the retry-policy integration, or choose a different error sentinel. Works for spike/exploration; needs rewrite for production.\n388\t\n389\tWhen in doubt, write **Level 3** and let audit suggest tightening to Level 2 if the agent would be likely to drift.\n390\t\n391\t### State the why, not just the what\n392\t\n393\t`<objective>` should answer *why this change matters* in one clause, not just what to build. Models reason better when they know the purpose — same effort, fewer wrong abstractions.\n394\t\n395\t```\n396\tBAD:  Add a validation command field to project config.\n397\tGOOD: Add a validation command field to project config so projects can enforce repo-specific checks (e.g. `make precommit`) before a prompt is marked complete.\n398\t```\n399\t\n400\tThe \"why\" also constrains the agent: if it considers a shortcut that breaks the stated purpose, it has a reason to reject it.\n401\t\n402\t### End with a self-check\n403\t\n404\tThe final instruction should make the agent prove the change before declaring done — a cheap verification layer that catches agents that skip `<verification>`. Pattern: *\"Before you finish, re-run `<verification>` and confirm it passes; walk each acceptance criterion against the change.\"* The `<verification>` block states the command; the self-check states that the agent must actually run it and verify the result before finishing.\n405\t\n406\t### Title comes from the first body H1\n407\t\n408\tThe commit subject for a prompt is derived from the first markdown `# ` heading in\n409\tthe body (falling back to the filename when there is none). `#`-prefixed lines\n410\tinside fenced code blocks (`` ``` ```` or `~~~`) are ignored, so a YAML or shell\n411\tcomment quoted in a code example will never become the commit subject. To control\n412\tthe commit subject explicitly, put a real `# Your Title` line at the very top of the\n413\tbody, above any fenced block.\n414\t\n415\t### Anchor by name, not line number\n416\t\n417\tLine numbers go stale when prior prompts edit the same file:\n418\t\n419\t```\n420\tBAD:  \"At line 231, wrap the error\"\n421\tGOOD: \"In ProcessQueue, after the autoSetQueuedStatus call, wrap the error\"\n422\t```\n423\t\n424\t### Copy constraints from spec\n425\t\n426\tThe agent has no memory between prompts. Every prompt must repeat the relevant constraints from the spec.\n427\t\n428\t### Hard blockers vs soft guidance\n429\t\n430\tNegative instructions (\"do NOT do X\") are correct for **hard never-conditions** — actions the agent must never take regardless of context (e.g. `Do NOT commit — dark-factory handles git`). Keep those explicit and unambiguous; positive rewording risks ambiguity about the boundary.\n431\t\n432\tFor **soft behavioral guidance**, prefer stating what to do instead: \"do not return markdown\" conflicts with the ask, while \"write it as smooth flowing text paragraphs\" leaves no ambiguity. Reserve \"do not\" for true blockers.\n433\t\n434\t### One concern per prompt\n435\t\n436\tSmaller prompts succeed more often. Group coupled behaviors together, but don't mix unrelated changes.\n437\t\n438\t### Repo-relative paths only\n439\t\n440\tDark-factory runs prompts in a Docker container. Never use absolute paths (`/Users/...`) or home-relative paths (`~/...`).\n441\t\n442\t**Exception**: Files under the mounted claude config use the in-container path `/home/node/.claude/plugins/...`, NOT the host path `~/.claude-yolo/plugins/...`. See [yolo-container-setup.md](../yolo-container-setup.md#project-workspace-mount).\n443\t\n444\t### Cross-repo references\n445\t\n446\tIf a prompt needs to reference code in a sibling repo (e.g., a library at `~/Documents/workspaces/time/`), the file is NOT accessible inside the container. Options:\n447\t\n448\t1. **Point to the vendored copy** — `vendor/github.com/bborbe/time/time_parse-time.go` (preferred when the dep is already vendored).\n449\t2. **Add an `extraMount`** in `.dark-factory.yaml` to mount the sibling repo.\n450\t3. **Inline the relevant snippet** in the prompt body.\n451\t\n452\tNever leave a host-absolute path hoping the agent will figure it out — the agent will get a \"file not found\" and fail.\n453\t\n454\t### Test the boundaries the new code crosses\n455\t\n456\tA broad class of bug: new code passes every unit test because the tests verify the **shape** of what was added, but fails at runtime because it crosses a **boundary** (library validator, parser, dispatch registry, serialization, subprocess, external service) and that boundary imposes a constraint the shape tests never exercise.\n457\t\n458\t**Rule:** For every boundary the new code crosses, at least one test must traverse that boundary with the new value. Ask: *what happens to this value after it leaves the code I just wrote?*\n459\t\n460\t**Root cause framing:** the problem is *missing integration tests* — tests that exercise the same code path production traffic takes. The \"validator test\" pattern below is a cheap, mechanically-enforceable subset.\n461\t\n462\t**Common boundaries and their contracts:**\n463\t\n464\t- **Library validators / parsers** — `Validate()`, `Parse()`, `Check()`, `MustX()` enforce regex / schema / range / format at runtime. The Go compiler does not check the underlying string.\n465\t- **Registries and dispatch tables** — adding a handler / operation / route is not enough; the dispatcher must find it via the production lookup path.\n466\t- **Serialization round-trips** — JSON / YAML / protobuf tags, nested types, zero-value handling. A round-trip test is the contract.\n467\t- **External service contracts** — Kafka operation names, Prometheus label regex, DNS labels, URL schemes, HTTP route patterns.\n468\t- **Subprocess interfaces** — argv, env vars, stdin/stdout shape, exit codes. A wrong flag name silently fails.\n469\t- **Build-time constraints** — build tags, `go:generate` directives, struct tags read by code generators.\n470\t\n471\t**Two acceptable levels:**\n472\t\n473\t1. **Unit-level contract test** (cheap; use this when a single-function validator/parser exists): call the boundary function directly on the new value — `Validate(ctx)`, `Parse(...)`, marshal+unmarshal. For grouped values of the same library type, a table test enumerating all values in the package is the canonical shape.\n474\t\n475\t   ```go\n476\t   // IMPORTANT: every new <TypeName> below MUST also appear in the Validate-all\n477\t   // test table in <matching_test.go>. The boundary contract is only enforced\n478\t   // at runtime — the test is the only pre-deploy guard.\n479\t   ```\n480\t\n481\t2. **Integration test through the real boundary** (more thorough; use this when no single-function validator exists, or when the change introduces a new integration seam — publish path, registry entry, new CLI flag): drive the new value through the real production path in a test harness.\n482\t\n483\t**Scope note:** E2E deployment verification (dev deploy + smoke tests) is a spec/scenario concern, not a prompt concern. Prompts cover unit and integration; specs cover acceptance; scenarios cover end-to-end.\n484\t\n485\t**What does NOT satisfy this rule:**\n486\t\n487\t- Struct equality tests (`Expect(cmd.TaskIdentifier).To(Equal(\"foo\"))`)\n488\t- Accessor-default tests (`Expect(fm.TriggerCount()).To(Equal(0))`)\n489\t- Constant-value tests (`Expect(string(OpX)).To(Equal(\"op-x\"))`) — these assert what you typed, not what the boundary accepts.\n490\t\n491\t**Why this rule exists:** in a real incident, `IncrementFrontmatterCommandOperation = \"increment_frontmatter\"` (underscores) passed every unit test but was rejected at runtime by the cqrs validator's regex `^[a-z][a-z-]*$`, causing a silent message-retry loop in dev that took a deploy cycle to diagnose. A six-line table test calling `.Validate(ctx)` on each declared operation would have caught it at `make precommit`.\n492\t\n493\t### Never run `go mod vendor` in a prompt\n494\t\n495\t`vendor/` is a build-time artifact: gitignored, regenerated by `make buca` before `docker build`. `make precommit`'s `ensure` target wipes it and uses `-mod=mod`. Dark-factory doesn't build Docker images, so nothing it does needs `vendor/`.\n496\t\n497\t**Never write `-mod=vendor` in a verification command.** bborbe repos don't commit `vendor/`, so `go test -mod=vendor …` (and `go tool cover` on its profile) fails with \"inconsistent vendoring\". Always use `-mod=mod` for `go test` / coverage verification commands.\n498\t\n499\tDep bump: `go get foo@v1.2.3 && go mod tidy` — that's it.\n500\t\n501\tException: if the repo commits `vendor/`, document that deviation in the prompt.\n502\t\n503\t### Add imports before tidying\n504\t\n505\t`go mod tidy` (and `make ensure`, which calls it) removes any direct entry from `go.mod` that no code currently imports. A prompt step that does `go get foo@v1.2.3 && go mod tidy` BEFORE the file that imports `foo` is written will silently demote/remove the dep — the next `make precommit` then fails on the unresolved import.\n506\t\n507\t```\n508\tBAD:  Step 1: go get foo && go mod tidy\n509\t      Step 2: write code that imports foo\n510\t      → Step 1 left go.mod unchanged because nothing imported foo yet\n511\tGOOD: Step 1: write code that imports foo\n512\t      Step 2: go mod tidy (promotes foo to a direct dep)\n513\t```\n514\t\n515\tFor prompts that add a new dependency: state the order explicitly — write the import first, then tidy.\n516\t\n517\t### `make ensure` vs `make precommit` mid-implementation\n518\t\n519\tWhen a prompt says \"add a new dependency\" or \"regenerate vendored code\", the intermediate state cannot pass `make precommit` (test+check fail until the code is updated). Use `make ensure` for dependency-only preparation steps, then reserve `make precommit` for final verification.\n520\t\n521\t```\n522\tBAD: Step 1: \"Run `make precommit` to add the dep.\"   # fails — tests reference code not yet written\n523\tGOOD: Step 1: \"Run `make ensure` to add the dep.\"     # deps only\n524\t      Step N (last): \"Run `make precommit`.\"          # full check at the end\n525\t```\n526\t\n527\t## Decomposition\n528\t\n529\tWhen breaking a spec into multiple prompts:\n530\t\n531\t**The grouping test:** \"Can these behaviors be verified independently?\" If not, group them.\n532\t\n533\t**The verifiability test:** \"Can I write a test that distinguishes before vs after?\" If not, merge or sharpen.\n534\t\n535\t**Sequencing:** Most foundational prompt first. Each prompt's postconditions become the next prompt's preconditions.\n536\t\n537\t| Feature size | Typical prompts |\n538\t|--------------|----------------|\n539\t| Config change | 1 |\n540\t| Single feature | 2-3 |\n541\t| Major feature | 4-6 |\n542\t| Full project bootstrap | 8-15 |\n543\t\n544\t## Prompt Definition of Done\n545\t\n546\tA prompt is ready for approval when ALL checks pass:\n547\t\n548\t**Structure:**\n549\t- [ ] All sections present: `<summary>`, `<objective>`, `<context>`, `<requirements>`, `<constraints>`, `<verification>`\n550\t- [ ] `<summary>` has 5-10 plain-language bullets (no file paths or jargon)\n551\t- [ ] `<objective>` states end state in 1-3 sentences\n552\t- [ ] `<requirements>` are numbered, specific, unambiguous\n553\t- [ ] `<constraints>` copied from spec\n554\t- [ ] `<verification>` has a runnable command\n555\t\n556\t**Accuracy:**\n557\t- [ ] File paths exist and are correct\n558\t- [ ] Function names and signatures match current code\n559\t- [ ] No stale references to renamed/moved/deleted code\n560\t- [ ] All paths are repo-relative\n561\t\n562\t**Quality:**\n563\t- [ ] Independently verifiable\n564\t- [ ] Libraries specified with import paths\n565\t- [ ] No duplicates with completed prompts\n566\t- [ ] Every boundary the new code crosses (library validator, parser, registry, serialization, subprocess, external service) has a test that traverses it with the new value (see \"Test the boundaries the new code crosses\" above)\n567\t\n568\t## Audit and Approve\n569\t\n570\tAlways audit before approving:\n571\t\n572\t```\n573\t/dark-factory:audit-prompt prompts/my-change.md\n574\t```\n575\t\n576\tThen approve via CLI (never manually edit frontmatter):\n577\t\n578\t```bash\n579\tdark-factory prompt approve my-change\n580\t```\n581\t\n582\tThis moves the prompt from `prompts/` to `prompts/in-progress/`, assigns a number, and sets `status: queued`.\n583\t\n584\t**Direct workflow:** Can approve multiple prompts at once — they execute sequentially on the same branch.\n585\t\n586\t**PR workflow:** Approve one at a time — each depends on the previous being merged.\n587\t\n588\t## Prompt Status Lifecycle\n589\t\n590\t| Status | Directory | Meaning | How it happens |\n591\t|--------|-----------|---------|----------------|\n592\t| `idea` | `prompts/ideas/` | Rough concept, needs refinement | Human creates file |\n593\t| `draft` | `prompts/` | Complete, ready for review and approval | Human/AI creates file |\n594\t| `approved` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Queued for execution | `dark-factory prompt approve` |\n595\t| `executing` | `prompts/in-progress/` | YOLO container running | Auto (dark-factory) |\n596\t| `committing` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Container succeeded, git commit pending | Auto (dark-factory) |\n597\t| `completed` | `prompts/completed/` | Done, archived | Auto (dark-factory) |\n598\t| `failed` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Needs fix or retry | Auto (dark-factory) |\n599\t| `cancelled` | `prompts/in-progress/` | Pulled before/during execution | `dark-factory prompt cancel <id>` |\n600\t\n601\tCompleted prompts are immutable. If behavior changes later, create a new prompt.\n602\t\n603\t### Fixing an Approved Prompt\n604\t\n605\tTo edit a prompt that has already been approved (or is executing) without recreating it:\n606\t\n607\t```bash\n608\tdark-factory prompt cancel <id>     # sets status: cancelled\n609\t# edit the .md file to fix issues\n610\tdark-factory prompt requeue <id>    # sets status: queued, re-enters the execution queue\n611\t```\n612\t\n613\tNote: `unapprove` does NOT work on `cancelled` prompts (error: \"only approved prompts can be unapproved\"). Use `requeue`.\n614\t\n615\tNever manually edit the `status:` frontmatter — always use the CLI commands.\n616\t\n617\t## Next Steps\n618\t\n619\t- Run the pipeline: [running.md](../running.md)\n620\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9412ecfd5e7d447b","session_id":"c245cf91-e27d-42c2-b8e2-b86a59610663","uuid":"22f57f50-4402-4d33-ab9c-6550179f411f","timestamp":"2026-08-27T18:29:54.182Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-creator","task_description":"Create prompts from spec 005"}
