{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a7f93a2ef40439a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Controller Design (task/controller)\n2\t\n3\tThe controller is the single writer to the vault git repo. It has two responsibilities: detecting task changes in git and publishing them to Kafka, and consuming commands from Kafka and writing results back to git. It has no K8s API access.\n4\t\n5\t## Inputs / Outputs\n6\t\n7\t| Direction | Topic | Purpose |\n8\t|-----------|-------|---------|\n9\t| Produces | `agent-task-v1-event` | Task created or status changed in git |\n10\t| Consumes | `agent-task-v1-request` | Update task commands (from agents) |\n11\t| Produces | `agent-task-v1-result` | Command processing confirmation (CQRS auto) |\n12\t\n13\t## Core Logic\n14\t\n15\t### 1. Change Detection (git → Kafka)\n16\t\n17\t```\n18\tPoll loop:\n19\t  │\n20\t  ├── Pull() — no-op (git-rest handles pulls internally)\n21\t  ├── gitClient.ListFiles(taskDir/*.md) → enumerate task files via HTTP\n22\t  ├── sha256-hash each file's content\n23\t  ├── compare with previous hashes\n24\t  │\n25\t  ├── changed file → parse frontmatter + body → publish agent-task-v1-event\n26\t  └── deleted file → publish agent-task-v1-event (deleted)\n27\t\n28\t> The scanner increments `agent_controller_vault_scanner_skipped_files_total{reason=<closed enum>}` at every skip site (broken frontmatter, unreadable file, empty status, injection failure, unresolvable duplicate frontmatter). The counter is pre-initialised at zero for every reason label so dashboards see all five before the first skip. Operators alert on `rate(agent_controller_vault_scanner_skipped_files_total[5m]) > 0`; a positive rate means a broken file is currently in the vault and is not being scanned.\n29\t\n30\t### 2. Command Processing (Kafka → git)\n31\t\n32\t```\n33\tOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"update\"):\n34\t  │\n35\t  ├── deserialize lib.Task from command payload\n36\t  ├── validate: TaskIdentifier and Content must be non-empty\n37\t  │\n38\t  ├── walk task directory, find file matching task_identifier in frontmatter\n39\t  ├── merge frontmatter + apply escalation check (counter set by executor, not incremented here)\n40\t  │     ├── read retry_count from merged frontmatter (set by executor at spawn time, spec 011)\n41\t  │     ├── if trigger_count >= max_triggers → clear assignee: \"\", preserve lifecycle phase, append ## Trigger Cap Escalation (once)\n42\t  │     ├── if retry_count >= max_retries   → clear assignee: \"\", preserve lifecycle phase, append ## Retry Escalation (once)\n43\t  │     └── if agent emits needs_input → clear assignee: \"\" (phase unchanged; spec-039 supersedes spec-021 for this row)\n44\t  ├── sanitize content (escape bare --- lines to prevent YAML corruption)\n45\t  ├── write frontmatter + content to file\n46\t  ├── git add + commit + push\n47\t  └── CQRS framework publishes success/failure result to agent-task-v1-result\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\tThe controller reads a required `VAULT_NAME` env var (CLI flag `--vault-name`) at startup naming the single Obsidian vault it serves. Every CreateCommand is checked against `VAULT_NAME` via the `pkg/routing.ShouldProcess` predicate: the effective target is `cmd.targetVault` if non-empty, otherwise the legacy fallback `openclaw`; commands whose effective target is not `VAULT_NAME` are skipped without side effects (no git write, no result publish, no error) and emit a single `glog.V(2)` line naming the command's `targetVault`, the effective target, and `VAULT_NAME` so operators can confirm routing decisions. Two controllers (e.g. one per vault) can therefore share the `agent-task-v1-request` topic without duplicating task materializations. The `targetVault` field is added to `task.CreateCommand` with `omitempty`; legacy producers that emit no `targetVault` continue to flow to the `openclaw` controller.\n51\t\n52\t## Frontmatter Merge\n53\t\n54\tWhen writing a result back, the ResultWriter merges frontmatter from the existing task file with frontmatter provided by the agent. Existing keys are preserved, agent keys override on conflict. This ensures fields like `assignee`, `tags`, and `task_identifier` survive result writeback even though agents don't receive frontmatter.\n55\t\n56\t```\n57\tExisting file:  {assignee: backtest-agent, tags: [agent-task], task_identifier: xyz}\n58\tAgent provides: {status: completed, phase: done}\n59\tMerged result:  {assignee: backtest-agent, tags: [agent-task], task_identifier: xyz, status: completed, phase: done}\n60\t```\n61\t\n62\t## Assignee-Clear on Escalation (spec 021, refined by spec 039, completed by spec 042)\n63\t\n64\tEvery escalation path writes `assignee: \"\"` so the task surfaces in operator inbox.\n65\tAll four rows route through the single chokepoint `result.ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview`\n66\t(for `human_review` paths) or `result.clearAssignee` (for cap paths) in\n67\t`task/controller/pkg/result/result_writer.go`:\n68\t\n69\t| Escalation trigger | `phase` written | `assignee` written | Enforcement point |\n70\t|---|---|---|---|\n71\t| `trigger_count >= max_triggers` | unchanged (lifecycle stage preserved) | `\"\"` | `applyTriggerCap` → `clearAssignee` |\n72\t| `retry_count >= max_retries` | unchanged (lifecycle stage preserved) | `\"\"` | `applyRetryCap` → `clearAssignee` |\n73\t| Agent emits `Result.NextPhase: human_review` (legitimate handoff) | `human_review` (from `resolveNextPhase`) | `\"\"` | `applyRetryCounter` → `ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview` |\n74\t| Agent emits `UpdateFrontmatterCommand` with merged `phase: human_review` (spec 042) | `human_review` | `\"\"` | `buildUpdateModifyFn` → `ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview` |\n75\t\n76\tOnce a task is parked (escalation section present, `assignee: \"\"`), repeated stale agent\n77\tresult publishes are idempotent: the escalation section is not duplicated, the lifecycle\n78\tphase is restored from the on-disk value, and assignee stays empty.\n79\t\n80\tThe `phase == \"human_review\"` assignee-clear guard in `resultWriter.applyRetryCounter`\n81\truns BEFORE the `spawn_notification` early return. This ordering is load-bearing: on\n82\ta pr-reviewer agent's first post-spawn write, the merged frontmatter carries\n83\t`spawn_notification: true` (inherited from the executor's spawn-time\n84\t`UpdateFrontmatterCommand`) AND incoming `phase: human_review` (from\n85\t`Result.NextPhase` via `resolveNextPhase`). The guard fires regardless of\n86\t`spawn_notification` state — see spec 041 for the 2026-05-25 prod incident reproducer\n87\tand prompt 075 for the same reorder pattern applied to `applyTriggerCap` on\n88\t2026-04-24.\n89\t\n90\t## Empty-to-Named Reset (spec 021)\n91\t\n92\tWhen the vault scanner observes a task file whose `assignee` transitions from empty (or absent) to a non-empty agent name, it writes `trigger_count: 0` and `retry_count: 0` back to the file atomically and queues a git commit. This refills the per-attempt budgets for the re-delegated agent without requiring manual counter edits. The reset fires exactly once per empty-to-named transition (named→named and named→empty transitions do not trigger a reset).\n93\t\n94\t## Atomic Frontmatter Commands\n95\t\n96\tIn addition to the `\"update\"` operation (full result write), the controller handles two atomic frontmatter operations on `agent-task-v1-request`:\n97\t\n98\t### `\"increment-frontmatter\"` (IncrementFrontmatterExecutor)\n99\t\n100\tPayload: `lib.IncrementFrontmatterCommand{TaskIdentifier, Field, Delta}`\n101\t\n102\t```\n103\tOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"increment-frontmatter\"):\n104\t  │\n105\t  ├── deserialize IncrementFrontmatterCommand\n106\t  ├── find task file by task_identifier (WalkDir)\n107\t  ├── if not found → log warning, return nil (no error)\n108\t  ├── AtomicReadModifyWriteAndCommitPush:\n109\t  │     ├── read current file bytes (under mutex)\n110\t  │     ├── parse frontmatter, read Field value (default 0 if absent)\n111\t  │     ├── newVal = currentVal + Delta\n112\t  │     ├── set Field = newVal\n113\t  │     ├── cap escalation: if Field == \"trigger_count\" AND newVal >= max_triggers\n114\t  │     │     └── clear assignee in the same write (phase unchanged; spec-039 supersedes spec-021 for this row)\n115\t  │     ├── write updated file (under mutex)\n116\t  │     └── git commit + push (under mutex)\n117\t  └── increment FrontmatterCommandsTotal{operation, outcome}\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tDelta may be negative (decrement). Cap escalation only fires for `trigger_count` reaching `max_triggers`.\n121\t\n122\t### `\"update-frontmatter\"` (UpdateFrontmatterExecutor)\n123\t\n124\tPayload: `lib.UpdateFrontmatterCommand{TaskIdentifier, Updates map[string]any}`\n125\t\n126\t```\n127\tOn agent-task-v1-request (operation: \"update-frontmatter\"):\n128\t  │\n129\t  ├── deserialize UpdateFrontmatterCommand\n130\t  ├── if Updates is empty → return nil (no-op, no write)\n131\t  ├── find task file by task_identifier (WalkDir)\n132\t  ├── if not found → log warning, return nil\n133\t  ├── AtomicReadModifyWriteAndCommitPush:\n134\t  │     ├── read current file bytes (under mutex)\n135\t  │     ├── parse existing frontmatter\n136\t  │     ├── merge only the keys in Updates (all other keys unchanged)\n137\t  │     ├── if Body section provided → append/replace section in body (spec 016)\n138\t  │     ├── if merged phase == \"human_review\" → result.ClearAssigneeIfHumanReview clears assignee in the same write (spec 042)\n139\t  │     ├── write updated file (under mutex)\n140\t  │     └── git commit + push (under mutex)\n141\t  └── increment FrontmatterCommandsTotal{operation, outcome}\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\t## Vault Writes via git-rest\n145\t\n146\tThe controller holds no local git clone. All vault file operations flow through the\n147\t`vault-obsidian-openclaw` git-rest StatefulSet via HTTP:\n148\t\n149\t| Operation | HTTP call | Who commits |\n150\t|-----------|-----------|-------------|\n151\t| Read file | `GET /api/v1/files/{relPath}` | N/A |\n152\t| Write file | `POST /api/v1/files/{relPath}` | git-rest (auto-commit) |\n153\t| Delete file | `DELETE /api/v1/files/{relPath}` | git-rest (auto-commit) |\n154\t| List files | `GET /api/v1/files/?glob={pattern}` | N/A |\n155\t\n156\tgit-rest ensures one commit per write. The controller's `/readiness` endpoint reflects\n157\tgit-rest readiness: if git-rest returns 503 (push stuck), the controller reports 503\n158\tand the Kafka consumer goroutine blocks inside the write retry loop until git-rest\n159\trecovers. Kafka offsets are not advanced during this block.\n160\t\n161\tBoltDB (at `/data/bolt` on the `datadir` PVC) continues to track Kafka consumer\n162\toffsets — unchanged from the pre-migration architecture.\n163\t\n164\t## Content Sanitization\n165\t\n166\tAgent output may contain bare `---` lines that would corrupt YAML frontmatter boundaries. The ResultWriter escapes these to `\\-\\-\\-` before writing.\n167\t\n168\t## HTTP Endpoints\n169\t\n170\t| Endpoint | Purpose |\n171\t|----------|---------|\n172\t| `/healthz` | Liveness probe |\n173\t| `/readiness` | Readiness probe |\n174\t| `/metrics` | Prometheus metrics |\n175\t| `/setloglevel` | Temporary log level change (5-min auto-reset) |\n176\t| `/trigger` | On-demand vault scan cycle |\n177\t\n178\t## What the Controller Does NOT Do\n179\t\n180\t- No K8s API calls (task/executor handles job spawning)\n181\t- No domain logic (doesn't know what a backtest is)\n182\t- No job management (doesn't know about pods)\n183\t- No prompt conversion (removed in v0.17.0)\n184\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9412ecfd5e7d447b","session_id":"c245cf91-e27d-42c2-b8e2-b86a59610663","uuid":"6e2c2f57-719a-4931-878c-51b149d8813c","timestamp":"2026-08-27T18:31:27.021Z","subagent_type":"dark-factory:prompt-creator","task_description":"Create prompts from spec 005"}
