er: lib.TaskFrontmatter{\"assignee\": \"claude\", \"status\": \"next\"}})`, handle once with `_, _, err := executor.HandleCommand(ctx, nil, cmdObj)`. **Positive (reopen):** stage `fakeGit.ReadFileReturns(...)`\n\n2. **(Spec failure-mode coverage, soft)** — Failure Mode row 5 (\"two create commands for the same title, different `task_identifier`, land concurrently on different Kafka partitions\") has no dedicated test in either prompt. The expected behavior (single file, never two files, never a lost live task) rests on the overwrite-in-place design decision plus the replay-idempotency test in prompt 1 (which covers the \"next sweep suppresses\" recovery half). This is defensible — the counterfeiter harness cannot realistically race two goroutines through one mock — but the DoD asks each failure trigger to map to a requirement. Consider a one-line note in the prompt set acknowledging row 5 is covered by design + the idempotency test, so the coverage decision is explicit rather than silent.\n\n3. **(Note, no change needed)** — The `<context>` OPEN QUESTION about the commit-message prefix is a good call, and the auditor confirms the chosen form `\"[agent-task-controller] reopen terminal task <id>\"` matches in-repo precedent: `transitionPrior` already uses `\"[agent-task-controller] auto-supersede prior recurring task \" + priorTitle` (`task_create_task_executor.go:491`), and the create path uses `\"[agent-task-controller] create task \"`. The choice is well-founded; the reviewer can close the question.\n\n## Strengths\n- Dependency gate is exemplary: the `<context>` starts with a grep that verifies prompt 1 shipped, and instructs a fail-loud `status: failed` with a named sentinel message if not — exactly the cross-prompt fail-loud discipline the DoD wants. The gate string (`reopened bool` in `task_create_task_executor.go`) matches what prompt 1's requirement 3 actually produces, so it is not a phantom check.\n- The `glog.go:520` line-number citation is exact, and the 