sting tests passed, so these two findings are purely the prompt's mandated state. An executor following Requirement 3 verbatim will fail the `<verification>`'s `make precommit`, and will be forced to invent a workaround (`_ = reopened`) that Requirement 3 explicitly forbids. (I also confirmed the \"result reopened is always false\" unparam finding from my stub is an artifact of my minimal reproduction — the full prompt body has a `return true, ...` branch, so it will not fire in the real implementation.)\n\n**Fix** (validated: after applying the below consumption, `make lint` returns clean): replace the sentence at line 192 —\n\n> The two new parameters are declared but not yet consumed — the observability that distinguishes a reopen (unconditional INFO log + distinct commit message) is specified by the follow-up prompt in this spec (Desired Behavior 5). Go permits unused function parameters; the linter does not flag them.\n\nwith:\n\n> The two new parameters MUST be referenced in the function body: the repo's `.golangci.yml` enables `unparam`, which flags declared-but-unused parameters in non-test code, and `make precommit` runs `make lint` over it (verified — `writeTaskFile - reopened is unused (unparam)`). Do NOT ship the observability here (the frozen `create-task: reopening terminal task` INFO log and the `reopen terminal task` commit message remain the follow-up prompt's scope); instead consume both params with a debug-only reference immediately before the `buildCreateTaskContent` call, which satisfies unparam and uses neither frozen substring so the follow-up prompt's grep ACs are unaffected:\n>\n> ```go\n> if reopened {\n>     glog.V(3).Infof(\"create-task: terminal reopen signal (prior status %s)\", priorStatus)\n> }\n> ```\n>\n> Keep the content, commit message, and the existing `glog.V(2).Infof(\"create-task: created task file at ...\")` line unchanged.\n\n(Alternative, cleanest resolution: the `<context>` OPEN QUESTION already floats merging prompts 1+2 — merging removes the declared-but-unused state entirely. If the observability ships atomically with the decision, the lint failure disappears and the frozen substrings are still correctly scoped.)\n\n### Critical 2 — `<verification>` \"Expect exactly 2\" is self-inconsistent with Requirement 7; the count will never be 2 after the mandated tests are added\n\n**Lines 258-261.** The verification runs:\n\n```\ncd /workspace && grep -c 'Expect(errors.Is(err, task.ErrTaskAlreadyExists)).To(BeTrue())' pkg/command/task_create_task_executor_test.go\n```\nExpect exactly `2` (the two pre-existing collision contexts' sentinel assertions, unmodified).\n\nRequirement 7 mandates new tests that assert the same sentinel with the same natural Gomega form: the non-terminal `DescribeTable` body, the ambiguous/unreadable `DescribeTable` body, and the replay-idempotency second-handle assertion. Each adds a textual occurrence of the exact grepped string. After the prompt's own tests are added the file contains **5** occurrences (2 pre-existing at lines 190/229 + 3 new), not 2. The check cannot produce its documented result, and a literal executor either reports a false verification failure or \"fixes\" it by contorting the new tests. `grep -c` over the whole file cannot distinguish \"pre-existing assertions unmodified\" from \"new assertions added\".\n\n**Fix** — scope the grep to the two adjacent, frozen collision contexts (Requirement 6 freezes them in place, so the anchors are stable). Replace lines 258-261 with:\n\n```\ncd /workspace && sed -n '/Context(\"title path already occupied (collision)\")/,/Context(\"transient git-rest read error\")/p' pkg/command/task_create_task_executor_test.go | grep -c 'Expect(errors.Is(err, task.ErrTaskAlreadyExists)).To(BeTrue())'\n```\nExpect exactly `2` (the two pre-existing collision contexts' sentinel assertions, unmodified). New test `Context` blocks added elsewhere in the file fall outside this range.\n\n(Simpler alternative: drop the count check entirely — `make precommit` behaviorally enforces both contexts' assertions, and the existing `grep -n 'must not consult frontmatter'` → no-match check already proves the comment edit. The spec's own AC evidence for \"pre-existing specs unmodified\" is `git diff`, which is operator-side by design.)\n\n## Recommendations\n\n1. **Spec failure-mode row 5 (two concurrent same-title, different-identifier creates) has no matching requirement in either prompt** (severity: Recommendation, not Critical — the spec explicitly documents last-write-wins as the *designed, reversible* outcome, and true concurrency is not unit-testable with the fakeGit harness). Add one proxy test: reopen a terminal file, then issue a create for the same title with a *different* `task_identifier` and assert `ErrTaskAlreadyExists` + write count unchanged. This locks the row's \"next sweep suppresses further writes because the file is now non-terminal\" tail, which the current replay-idempotency test (same identifier) does not cover.\n\n2. **Summary jargon nit (line 12)**: bullet 2 uses the sentinel name `ErrTaskAlreadyExists` verbatim. The summary guidance asks for plain language for the human reviewer — suggest \"is still dropped with the existing 'task already exists' failure\". Minor.\n\n3. **Verification count-2 context (Requirement 7)**: the \"completed → reopen\" test asserts `ReadFileCallCount() == 1`, which depends on `supersedePriorRecurringTask` being a no-op for the test command (no `created_by`). This is correct as written, but it is a silent coupling — a one-line note in the test that the single-read lock holds only because the command is not supersede-eligible would prevent a future executor from \"helpfully\" adding `created_by` and breaking the assertion.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- Every code reference is accurate — I verified all line numbers, signatures, mock shapes, module symbols (including `TaskFrontmatter.String` semantics at `agent_task-frontmatter.go:144`), and the go.mod module 